    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Iran Country Coordinating Mechanism (CCM)

The Iran CCM is a multi-stakeholder body responsible for overseeing Global Fund grants aimed at combating HIV, tuberculosis (TB), and malaria. It includes representatives from government, civil society, academic institutions, UN agencies, the private sector, and people living with or affected by these diseases.
Core Responsibilities:
Development of Funding Requests: Coordinates the preparation and submission of national funding proposals to the Global Fund, ensuring alignment with national health priorities.
Nomination of Principal Recipients (PRs): Selects and nominates organizations to manage and implement approved grants.
Grant Oversight: Monitors the implementation of grants to ensure compliance with agreed-upon objectives and standards.
Approval of Reprogramming Requests: Evaluates and approves any modifications to grant activities or budgets.
Ensuring Alignment with National Programs: Facilitates coordination between Global Fund-supported initiatives and other national health and development programs.

Oversight Functions:
Establishment of Oversight Committee: The Iran CCM has established an Oversight Committee to plan, coordinate, and carry out oversight of Global Fund grants. This committee provides evidence-based recommendations to the CCM for discussion and decision-making. 
Regular Monitoring: Conducts field visits and meetings with Principal and Sub-Recipients to assess program performance and address any challenges.
Stakeholder Engagement: Engages program stakeholders, including CCM members and non-members, particularly non-government constituencies and people living with or affected by the diseases, in oversight activities. 
Reporting and Accountability: Documents observations and shares findings with the broader CCM and relevant authorities, ensuring transparency and accountability in grant implementation.
Through these activities, the Iran CCM ensures that Global Fund resources are utilized effectively and equitably, contributing to the national response to HIV, TB, and malaria.</narrative>
    </description>
